[h1]Intercolony[/h1]

Intercolony attempts to turn Rimworld from a colony manager into a trading, produce to sell, procure to produce style manager. It make resources more flexible, acquisition of stuff simpler and selling stuff more satisfying.

You might become a nomad, delivering sought after goods while moving from colony to colony, buying cheap and selling to the highest bidder - importing rare resources from far away lands, selling it at a premium to the locals whomever pays most.

You might make a furniture factory, re-order raw resources whenever you run low and set-up recurring sales to settlements that have grown to trust you. Nurture a tranquil colony that doesn't want to cause trouble with anyone else... and use the proceeds to hire workers and defenders of this safe haven you've created - who knows, some of them might even want to stay.

You might conquer a vast amount of silver, use your conquered wealth to form a militia of disposable, loyal, pre-payed mercenaries, and raid your enemy ruthlessly - sell their goods and move on to the next settlement! Order the muscle and the equipment from the world market and rain hellfire on your opponents.

You might mix this up with other mods like hospitality to help you maintain your Rimworld hotel without the need to venture outside of your settlement constantly because there are no components left in the map and you haven't received a trader in what seems like a decade...

You can buy whatever.
You can sell whatever.
Livestock included - buy a breeding pair by specification, or sell off the flock you can't feed.

You can sustain almost any playstyle without needing to do intense playstyle gymnastics...

If you like your game to get into a rhythm faster, if you like specialisation of a settlement, you might really like this.

[hr][/hr]
[h1]What it adds[/h1]

A new [b]intercolony[/b] tab on the bottom bar, with five screens:

[list]
[*][b]Business[/b] — where you stand. Silver in storage, the daily wage bill across your workforce, what moved over the last quadrum or year, and the margin on your standing agreements.
[*][b]Selling[/b] — the [i]Market[/i] of what settlements currently want; [i]Find buyer[/i] to locate someone who wants stock you already have; [i]Orders[/i] for what you have committed to; [i]Contracts[/i] for recurring supply agreements.
[*][b]Procurement[/b] — request goods you cannot make, collect quotations from settlements that can supply them, and buy from whoever answers best. Pickup or delivery.
[*][b]Labor[/b] — [i]Hire[/i] a worker directly, put up a public [i]Post[/i] that stays up until you take it down and hire as many applicants as answer it, and manage your [i]Employees[/i].
[*][b]Relations[/b] — the commercial record you have built with each settlement (NOT THE SAME AS GOODWILL!!!).
[/list]

[h1]Orders[/h1]

Demand commodities, intermediate goods, manufactured products, furniture, art, weapons, apparel, live animals and almost anything thats tradeable and even somethings that are not (special item trading can be tailored in the settings). About the only thing that will never move is a building bolted to the floor - a caravan physically cannot carry it.

Deliveries and defaults build a commercial reputation, and once a settlement trusts you, you can negotiate a standing arrangement.

[h1]Employees[/h1]

Hired workers join your faction for the term and then leave. In between they are an obligation, if anything happens to them their home settlement will blame you for it... unless you hired a mercenary... then they wont blame you as much - they still wont be pleased one of their colonists got killed in your (possibly suicide) mission.

Well treated workers might want to stay, or renew the contract - you get good things from treating them well, like cheaper future hires due to your decent employer fame... or the inverse, if you consistently put your employees in harms way and their contract terms don't allow for it.

[hr][/hr]
[h1]Requirements[/h1]

[list]
[*]RimWorld [b]1.6[/b]
[*][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=2009463077][b]Harmony[/b][/url], loaded [b]before[/b] Intercolony
[/list]

[h1]This is a beta — what that means[/h1]

I tested this mod intensely... that being said, I cant guarantee with a million percent certainty that it will behave nicely with your other mods... I tested it with my mods and had no issues.

[h1]The tests I made[/h1]

[list]
[*][b]RimWorld 1.6.4871 rev590.[/b]
[*][b]DLC: Biotech only.[/b] Royalty, Ideology and Anomaly are untested.
[*][b]Alongside:[/b] Hospitality, Common Sense, RT Fuse, Tilled Soil, and FSF Filth Vanishes With Rain And Time.
[*][b]Employees are quest lodgers in your faction.[/b] Mods that assume "player faction" means "permanent colonist" — colonist bars, work-tab replacements, roster mods — are the main compatibility risk, and none has been tried.
[/list]

[h1]Found a problem?[/h1]

Please report it on the [url=https://github.com/miannoni/Intercolony/issues]GitHub issue tracker[/url] or in the comments, I check them often and a few feedbacks have already been incorporated. The more detail the better.

I would like to know what you did with the mod, so whatever you feel like sharing -- please do!

